=== THE REAL EXPERIMENT: bare dsec, NO hint, domain_b vs domain_b_onehot ===
=== (decides whether branch 4 / mapped-state equivalence is needed at all) ===
$ yosys-abc -c "cec  work/domain_b.aig work/domain_b_onehot.aig"
Networks have different number of latches.
Miter computation has failed.

$ yosys-abc -c "dsec work/domain_b.aig work/domain_b_onehot.aig"
Warning: 90 registers in this network have don't-care init values.
The don't-care are assumed to be 0. The result may not verify.
Networks are NOT EQUIVALENT after simulation.   Time =     0.08 sec

VERDICT: cec cannot even build the miter (latch count mismatch, 42 vs 48).
dsec builds it and answers NOT EQUIVALENT in 0.08s -- a fast, clean, correct
"no" from a checker with no notion of a state-mapping abstraction. Same
qualitative signature as the toy MAC's latency-changing case (cec_check/),
different underlying reason (encoding, not latency). This is the measured
evidence that branch 4 (mapped-state equivalence, invariant supplied) is
necessary here, not redundant scaffolding.

Export recipe note: async2sync is required before dffunmap/techmap/aigmap
for this design -- domain_b/domain_b_onehot use genuine async resets
(posedge clk_div or negedge rst_n), which the toy MAC's synchronous-reset
files never exercised through this AIG-export path. Without it, aigmap fails
on the gate-level $_DFF_PN0_ primitive regardless of dffunmap ordering.
